.Dd November 3, 2026
.Dt IOCTL 2
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm ioctl
.Nd control a device
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In sys/ioctl.h
.Ft int
.Fn ioctl "int fd" "int request" "..."
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Fn ioctl
performs
.Fa request
on the object referred to by
.Fa fd .
The third argument, called
.Fa arg
below, is a pointer or an integer as the request requires.
The i386 system call number is 54: EBX is the descriptor, ECX the request,
and EDX the argument.
On x86-64 the number is 16, with the three in RDI, RSI and RDX.
.Pp
The descriptor is checked before the request.
Every descriptor not opened with
.Dv O_PATH
accepts
.Dv FIONBIO :
.Fa arg
points to an
.Vt int ;
nonzero sets
.Dv O_NONBLOCK
in the open file description, as
.Xr fcntl 2
does with
.Dv F_SETFL ,
and zero clears it.
They also accept
.Dv FIOCLEX ,
which sets the descriptor's close-on-exec flag, and
.Dv FIONCLEX ,
which clears it, as
.Xr fcntl 2
does with
.Dv F_SETFD ;
both ignore
.Fa arg .
Sockets first handle network-interface requests.
The console and pseudo-terminal slaves share the terminal requests below.
A descriptor with device operations handles its remaining requests through
those operations; the framebuffer and console handle their private requests.
An unsupported request returns
.Er ENOTTY .
The mouse has no requests of its own.
.Ss Terminal requests
The console and pseudo-terminal slaves accept:
.Bl -tag -width "TIOCGWINSZ"
.It Dv TCGETS
Copy the 36-byte kernel termios to
.Fa arg .
This is the kernel layout, the same on i386 and x86-64, with 19 control
characters, not musl's
larger termios structure with 32 control characters and speed fields.
A new terminal has Linux's control flags,
.Dv B38400 | CS8 | CREAD | HUPCL ,
and
.Dv IXON
among its input flags, with
.Dv VSTART
^Q and
.Dv VSTOP
^S.
.It Dv TCSETS , TCSETSW , TCSETSF
Read a 36-byte kernel termios from
.Fa arg
and install it.
.Dv TCSETSW
acts immediately, like
.Dv TCSETS .
.Dv TCSETSF
also discards unread input.
.It Dv TCGETS2
Copy the 44-byte termios2 to
.Fa arg :
the kernel termios, then the input and output speeds as unsigned
integers.
The output speed is the one the baud code in
.Va c_cflag
names, and the input speed the one its input code names, or the output
speed when that code is zero.
.Dv BOTHER ,
a speed given by number, reads as 0.
GNU libc's
.Fn tcgetattr
and
.Fn isatty
make this request.
.It Dv TCSETS2 , TCSETSW2 , TCSETSF2
Read a 44-byte termios2 from
.Fa arg
and install its termios as
.Dv TCSETS ,
.Dv TCSETSW
and
.Dv TCSETSF
do.
The speeds are not kept, since no terminal here has a line speed;
Linux keeps them.
GNU libc's
.Fn tcsetattr
makes these requests.
.It Dv TCFLSH
The integer
.Fa arg
selects the queue:
.Dv TCIFLUSH
(0) and
.Dv TCIOFLUSH
(2) discard unread input.
.Dv TCOFLUSH
(1) and
.Dv TCIOFLUSH
discard a pseudo-terminal slave's output its master has not read; the
console writes its output at once, so there they do nothing.
Other values are invalid.
.It Dv TCXONC
The integer
.Fa arg
selects the action, as
.Xr tcflow 3
gives it:
.Dv TCOOFF
(0) stops the terminal's output and
.Dv TCOON
(1) restarts it; a stop made so outlasts
.Dv VSTART
and
.Dv IXANY ,
which end only a stop that
.Dv VSTOP
made.
.Dv TCIOFF
(2) and
.Dv TCION
(3) send the
.Dv VSTOP
and
.Dv VSTART
characters as output, also while output is stopped by
.Dv VSTOP .
While it is stopped by
.Dv TCOOFF ,
the console still sends them, as Linux's console and serial line do, and
a pseudo-terminal slave sends nothing, as Linux's does;
a disabled character sends nothing.
Other values are
.Er EINVAL .
While output is stopped, a write waits, or with
.Dv O_NONBLOCK
fails with
.Er EAGAIN
once it has written nothing, and
.Xr poll 2
gives no
.Dv POLLOUT .
On the console a stop lights Scroll Lock, and the Hold key, Scroll Lock,
stops and restarts output, as on Linux.
.It Dv FIONREAD
Write an
.Vt int
count of immediately readable bytes through
.Fa arg .
The input source is drained first.
Both modes report the bytes queued for reading, uncommitted canonical
input excluded; a pending end-of-file does not make the count zero.
.It Dv TIOCGWINSZ , TIOCSWINSZ
Read or replace the terminal's
.Vt "struct winsize"
through
.Fa arg .
Setting it through the console or slave does not send
.Dv SIGWINCH .
.It Dv TIOCGPGRP
Write the foreground process group ID through the
.Vt int
pointer
.Fa arg .
.It Dv TIOCSPGRP
Read a positive process group ID through the
.Vt int
pointer
.Fa arg
and make it foreground.
The terminal must belong to the caller's session, and the group must
have a member in that session.
If the number names no group but names a thread in the session, that
number is accepted as on Linux.
A negative number fails with
.Er EINVAL ;
zero or a number no thread, process group or session holds fails with
.Er ESRCH .
.It Dv TIOCSCTTY
Make this the session's controlling terminal and set its foreground
group to the caller's process group.
The caller must be a session leader; a terminal belonging to another
session cannot be taken.
Other terminals belonging to this session are detached.
Reclaiming the same terminal is permitted.
.Fa arg
is ignored and cannot force a takeover.
A session leader without a controlling terminal also acquires a
pseudo-terminal slave no session has by opening it without
.Dv O_NOCTTY ,
as on Linux.
.It Dv TIOCNOTTY
Detach the terminal if it belongs to the caller's session.
Otherwise succeed without action.
.El
.Pp
A process of a background group of the session a terminal controls that
calls
.Dv TCSETS
or its kin,
.Dv TCFLSH ,
.Dv TCXONC
or
.Dv TIOCSPGRP
on it is stopped first, as on Linux: the request posts
.Dv SIGTTOU
to its process group and fails with
.Er EINTR ,
to be restarted once the group is continued.
A process that blocks or ignores
.Dv SIGTTOU
makes the request at once.
A request that would post
.Dv SIGTTOU
from an orphaned process group, which no member's parent in the session
can continue, fails instead with
.Er EIO ,
and
.Dv TIOCSPGRP
with
.Er ENOTTY .
.Pp
The console alone also answers Linux's keyboard requests of
.In linux/kd.h ,
.Dv KDGKBTYPE ,
.Dv KDGKBMODE
and
.Dv KDSKBMODE ,
.Dv KDGKBMETA
and
.Dv KDSKBMETA ,
.Dv KDGKBLED
and
.Dv KDSKBLED ,
.Dv KDGETLED
and
.Dv KDSETLED ,
.Dv KDGKBENT
and
.Dv KDSKBENT ,
.Dv KDGKBSENT
and
.Dv KDSKBSENT ,
.Dv KDGKBDIACR ,
.Dv KDSKBDIACR ,
.Dv KDGKBDIACRUC
and
.Dv KDSKBDIACRUC ,
.Dv KDKBDREP
and
.Dv KDGETMODE ,
as
.Xr console 4
describes them, with who may set what; a pseudo-terminal slave answers
them with
.Er ENOTTY .
.Ss Pipes and pseudo-terminals
Pipes and pseudo-terminal masters accept
.Dv FIONREAD
to return their queued byte count through an
.Vt int
pointer.
.Pp
A pseudo-terminal master additionally accepts:
.Bl -tag -width "TIOCSPTLCK"
.It Dv TIOCGPTN
Write the pseudo-terminal number through an
.Vt int
pointer.
.It Dv TIOCSPTLCK
Read an
.Vt int
through
.Fa arg .
Nonzero locks the slave; zero unlocks it.
.It Dv TIOCGWINSZ , TIOCSWINSZ
Read or replace the slave's window size.
Setting it through the master sends
.Dv SIGWINCH
to the slave's foreground group, if one is set.
.It Dv TIOCPKT , TIOCGPKT
Set packet mode from an
.Vt int ,
nonzero on, or read it back.
In packet mode each read of the master begins with a status byte,
.Dv TIOCPKT_DATA ,
0, before the slave's output, as screen asks for.
A flush on the slave makes the master readable, with
.Dv POLLPRI
as well, for a status byte alone:
.Dv TIOCPKT_FLUSHREAD
after its input is discarded by
.Dv TCFLSH
or
.Dv TCSETSF ,
and
.Dv TIOCPKT_FLUSHWRITE
after its output is discarded by
.Dv TCFLSH ,
or both, as a signal character does unless
.Dv NOFLSH
is set.
.Dv TIOCPKT_STOP
and
.Dv TIOCPKT_START
follow a stop and a restart of the slave's output, and
.Dv TIOCPKT_NOSTOP
and
.Dv TIOCPKT_DOSTOP
a termios change that ends or begins flow control by ^S and ^Q, that is
.Dv IXON
with those two characters.
A slave's
.Xr vhangup 2
leaves no status pending.
.It Dv TCFLSH
With
.Fa arg
0, drop the slave's output the master has not read; with 1, the input the
slave has not read; with 2, both.
.It Dv TCXONC
.Dv TCOOFF
stops the master's writes, which wait, or fail with
.Er EAGAIN
under
.Dv O_NONBLOCK ,
until
.Dv TCOON ;
.Dv TCIOFF
and
.Dv TCION
feed ^S and ^Q to the slave as input, unless the master's writes are
stopped.
.El
.Pp
A master's write goes on after every slave description has closed, as
Linux's does, until the slave's input is full.
.Ss Disk requests
Raw disk windows and loop devices with a file attached accept:
.Bl -tag -width "BLKGETSIZE64"
.It Dv BLKGETSIZE64
Write the window's byte size as an eight-byte unsigned integer through
.Fa arg .
Both request numbers 0x80041272 and 0x80081272 are accepted.
.It Dv BLKGETSIZE
Request 0x1260 writes the count of 512-byte sectors as a 32-bit unsigned
integer through
.Fa arg .
A count larger than 4294967295 is refused.
.It Dv BLKSSZGET , BLKPBSZGET
Requests 0x1268 and 0x127b write the logical and the physical sector
size, 512, as an int.
.It Dv BLKROGET , BLKRAGET , BLKBSZGET
Requests 0x125e, 0x1263 and 0x80041270 (0x80081270 from a 64-bit header)
write, as an int, 1 for a loop device attached read-only and 0 otherwise,
a read-ahead of 256 sectors, and a block size of 4096, which
.Ic blockdev
reports.
.It Dv BLKRRPART
Request 0x125f rereads the partition table on a whole-disk node.
A partition node does not accept it.
The rescan is refused while the disk has another open descriptor or
a mounted partition.
.Fa arg
is ignored.
.It Dv HDIO_GETGEO
Request 0x0301 writes the eight-byte
.Vt struct hd_geometry :
255 heads, 63 sectors a track, the cylinders those give cut to 16 bits,
and the window's first sector on its disk, 0 for a whole disk, as Linux
makes up a geometry for a disk that has none.
BusyBox's
.Xr fdisk 8
reads the start to tell a whole disk from a partition, since a whole
disk's name here ends in a digit.
.El
.Ss File attribute requests
A file or a directory of an ext2 volume accepts the requests of
.Xr chattr 8
and
.Xr lsattr 8 ;
a file of another type answers
.Er ENOTTY ,
as Linux does for a file system that keeps no flags.
.Bl -tag -width "FS_IOC_GETVERSION"
.It Dv FS_IOC_GETFLAGS
Request 0x80046601 writes the inode's flags, the bits of
.Ql i_flags
that Linux shows, as a 32-bit integer through
.Fa arg .
.It Dv FS_IOC_SETFLAGS
Request 0x40046602 sets the flags ext2 lets a user change from the
integer at
.Fa arg ,
for the file's owner or a privileged caller; only a privileged caller
changes the immutable flag, 0x10, and the append-only flag, 0x20, as
.Dv CAP_LINUX_IMMUTABLE
does on Linux.
The kernel enforces those two as Linux does: an immutable file refuses a
write, a truncation, a removal, a rename, a link and a change of its
attributes, to root too, and an append-only file refuses all of them but
a write at its end, through a descriptor opened with
.Dv O_APPEND .
.It Dv FS_IOC_GETVERSION
Request 0x80047601 writes the inode's generation number as a 32-bit
integer through
.Fa arg .
.El
.Ss Clone requests
.Dv FICLONE ,
0x40049409, whose
.Fa arg
is a source descriptor, and
.Dv FICLONERANGE ,
0x4020940d, whose
.Fa arg
points to a
.Vt struct file_clone_range
that begins with the source descriptor, ask that the destination
.Fa fd
share the source's extents, as
.Ql cp --reflink
asks.
Every descriptor takes them, and they fail as Linux's do on a file
system that shares no extents: with
.Er EBADF
for a source not open,
.Er EXDEV
for two file systems,
.Er EISDIR
for a directory,
.Er EINVAL
for anything else that is not a regular file,
.Er EBADF
for a source not open for reading or a destination not open for writing
or with
.Dv O_APPEND ,
and otherwise
.Er EOPNOTSUPP ,
since no file system here shares extents.
.Ql cp --reflink=auto
copies the bytes after that.
.Ss Framebuffer requests
A framebuffer descriptor accepts the private requests below, whose
numbers and structures are in
.In rach2/fb.h .
Every request but
.Dv FB_MODE_13H
on a display nobody owns requires ownership by the calling process.
A stale or inherited descriptor, such as one a child got by
.Xr fork 2 ,
cannot use another process's display; the request returns
.Er EBUSY .
.Bl -tag -width "FB_MODE_NATIVE"
.It Dv FB_MODE_13H No (0x4601)
Acquire an unowned display and enter mode 13h.
If the caller already owns the display in mode 13h, preserve its
contents and palette; from the native mode, go straight to mode 13h.
.It Dv FB_MODE_TEXT No (0x4602)
Release the calling owner and return to the suspended owner, if any.
Restore its mode, palette, keyboard settings and console termios, flush
unread input, and queue
.Dv FB_EVENT_REGAINED .
The receiving owner redraws its whole screen.
With no suspended owner, restore the original console settings and
redraw its saved text.
An unowned display gives
.Er EBUSY .
.It Dv FB_SETPALETTE No (0x4603)
Read 768 bytes from
.Fa arg
as 256 RGB triples and set mode 13h's palette, in either mode.
.It Dv FB_GETINFO No (0x4604)
Write the framebuffer's
.Vt struct fb_screen
through
.Fa arg ,
whatever the mode: its width and height in pixels, its pitch, the bytes
from one row to the next, its bits a pixel, its kind,
.Dv FB_KIND_RGB
or
.Dv FB_KIND_INDEXED ,
and for RGB each channel's offset and width in bits, in the pixel taken
as a little-endian number.
A console on the VGA's text mode, which has no framebuffer, gives
.Er ENODEV .
.It Dv FB_MODE_NATIVE No (0x4605)
Enter the native mode: the whole screen at the size and pixel format
.Dv FB_GETINFO
reports, painted black, which the owner writes in rows of pixels at byte
offsets from the top left pixel, as
.Xr write 2
describes.
The console draws nothing until the owner leaves the mode, closes the
descriptor or exits.
A console on the VGA's text mode, and a framebuffer that is not RGB at
15, 16, 24 or 32 bits a pixel, give
.Er ENODEV .
.El
.Pp
A request that changes the mode, or takes the screen back with
.Dv FB_MODE_13H ,
puts the descriptor's offset back at 0.
.Pp
On a machine whose console is on a framebuffer, as one booted by UEFI is,
there is no VGA to put in mode 13h.
The kernel keeps the 320 by 200 picture in memory and draws each frame
written to the descriptor on the framebuffer, at the largest whole number
of the screen's pixels to one of its own that fits, three on a 1366 by 768
screen, in the middle on black, with the palette's colors.
The console draws nothing while the picture has the screen, and draws its
text again when the mode returns to text.
.Ss Console sound requests
These requests require a console descriptor.
They are not selected merely by descriptor number 1 or 2.
.Bl -tag -width "AUDIO_SET_VOLUME"
.It Dv KIOCSOUND No (0x4B2F)
Sound the PC speaker until the next request, at 1193182 Hz, the clock of
the PC's interval timer, divided by the integer argument, as on Linux.
A zero argument silences the speaker.
.It Dv KDMKTONE No (0x4B30)
Sound a tone and end it: the argument's high 16 bits are its length in
milliseconds and its low 16 bits the divisor, as for
.Dv KIOCSOUND .
The kernel's tick silences the speaker when the length has passed.
A length or divisor of 0 silences the speaker.
.Pp
Each of the two ends the tone or sound before it, and a divisor whose
pitch is not between 21 and 32766 Hz silences the speaker, as on Linux.
Where the HD Audio codec has a beep generator, each request sounds it
beside the PC speaker, as Linux sounds both: at 48000 / (4 x
.Ar d )
Hz, where the divider
.Ar d
is 12000 over the pitch, held between 1 and 255, so that 750 Hz sounds
as 750 Hz, and silent while the
.Dv SOUND_MIXER_SPEAKER
level of
.Xr mixer 4
is 0.
With no stream playing, a tone turns the codec's output pins on, and the
kernel turns them off a second after the generator stops.
Both return 0.
.Pa /proc/console
counts the tones and sounds; see
.Xr proc 5 .
.It Dv AUDIO_GET_INFO No (0x4101)
Write two 32-bit unsigned fields through
.Fa arg :
the driver type (0 none, 1 PC speaker, 2 Sound Blaster 16, 3 Intel HDA)
and whether sample output is available (0 or 1).
A null argument succeeds without writing.
.It Dv AUDIO_START No (0x4102)
Read three 32-bit unsigned fields through
.Fa arg :
sample rate in samples per second, format (0 unsigned 8-bit, 1 signed
16-bit little-endian), and channels (1 mono, 2 stereo).
On HD Audio and the Sound Blaster 16 the console plays on a channel of its
own in the mix that every
.Pa /dev/dsp
open plays in, as
.Xr dsp 4
describes, so neither waits for the other.
A start replaces the console's channel if it plays.
The channel takes a rate from 1 through 192000 Hz, held to at least 1000
and at most four times the mix's rate, and converts it to the mix's.
.Pa /proc/audio
shows the device's stream.
.It Dv AUDIO_STOP No (0x4103)
Stop the console's channel; every
.Pa /dev/dsp
open plays on.
.It Dv AUDIO_WRITE No (0x4104)
Read a structure containing a sample-buffer pointer followed by a
32-bit unsigned byte count through
.Fa arg .
Validate the buffer and pass it to the active driver.
The return value is the byte count queued in the console channel's ring of
three periods, which may be less than requested or zero when the ring is
full.
The caller must retry bytes that were not accepted.
The channel plays silence while the ring is empty, and the frames queued
after it play in order.
.It Dv AUDIO_SET_VOLUME No (0x4105)
Pass the integer argument to the active driver as a percentage from
0 through 100.
The sample drivers clamp it to that range; the PC speaker ignores it.
.El
.Ss Network-interface requests
A socket descriptor of any family, an
.Dv AF_UNIX
one included, as glibc's
.Xr if_nametoindex 3
opens, accepts interface queries using a
.Vt "struct ifreq"
in the processor's layout, 32 bytes on i386 and 40 on x86-64.
.Dv SIOCGIFCONF
fills the buffer of a
.Vt "struct ifconf"
with an ifreq, a name and an IPv4 address, for each interface that has an
address, and sets its length to the bytes filled; with no buffer it sets the
length all of them would take.
.Dv SIOCGIFNAME
takes an interface index and returns its name.
The other queries select the interface by name:
.Dv SIOCGIFINDEX ,
.Dv SIOCGIFHWADDR ,
.Dv SIOCGIFADDR ,
.Dv SIOCGIFNETMASK ,
.Dv SIOCGIFBRDADDR ,
.Dv SIOCGIFMTU ,
and
.Dv SIOCGIFFLAGS
return its index, hardware address, IPv4 address, netmask, broadcast
address, MTU, and flags, respectively.
.Dv SIOCGIWNAME
returns
.Er EOPNOTSUPP ;
wireless stations use nl80211 instead of wireless extensions.
.Pp
.Dv SIOCSIFADDR ,
.Dv SIOCSIFNETMASK ,
.Dv SIOCSIFBRDADDR ,
.Dv SIOCSIFFLAGS ,
and
.Dv SIOCSIFMTU
require effective user ID 0.
They set the address or netmask, check the derived broadcast address,
change the interface's
.Dv IFF_UP
state, or set its MTU.
A card takes an MTU from 68 to 1500 and
.Li lo
only 65536; any other is
.Er EINVAL .
In a network namespace other than the first, whose one interface is its
own
.Ql lo ,
privilege in the user namespace that owns it is enough, and they take
.Ql lo
up or down, the address 127.0.0.1 and the netmask 255.0.0.0 alone.
Other flag bits are ignored by the setter.
An address set without its mask uses a provisional classful mask until
the separate netmask request.
If the address is a network or broadcast address under the provisional
mask, the interface remains unconfigured until a valid netmask is
supplied.
A netmask request that fails with
.Er EINVAL ,
for a mask that is not contiguous, is all ones, or makes the address a
network or broadcast address, unconfigures the interface when the mask is
provisional or an address is pending, and the address must be set again.
Repeating the existing configured address leaves its mask unchanged.
Changing any interface's address leaves every TCP connection and
listening socket as it is, as on Linux.
A connection whose local address is no longer configured sends nothing
until the address returns, when it resumes; if it does not return, the
connection times out with
.Er ENETUNREACH ;
see
.Xr tcp 7 .
The change invalidates ARP mappings and clears a gateway that is no
longer on the interface's subnet.
.Pp
.Dv SIOCADDRT
and
.Dv SIOCDELRT
take a
.Vt "struct rtentry"
in the processor's layout, 84 bytes on i386 and 120 on x86-64,
and require effective user ID 0.
A default route of no metric sets or removes the selected interface's
default gateway; any other route is added to or removed from the table
of 32, with the metric one less than
.Va rt_metric ,
as
.Ic route
sends it.
A null device-name pointer selects the interface whose subnet has the
gateway, or the first.
Adding the interface's existing subnet route succeeds without action, and
removing it is
.Er EOPNOTSUPP
while its address is in force.
A default route of no metric and no gateway is
.Er EINVAL
and leaves the gateway as it was.
A gateway none of the interface's subnets has is
.Er ENETUNREACH ,
a route already there
.Er EEXIST ,
and removing one that is not there
.Er ESRCH .
.Pp
On an
.Dv AF_INET6
socket the four setters are IPv6's, as BusyBox's
.Ql ifconfig eth0 add ADDR/LEN
and
.Ql route -A inet6
send them, and require effective user ID 0.
.Dv SIOCSIFADDR
and
.Dv SIOCDIFADDR
(0x8936) take a 24-byte
.Vt "struct in6_ifreq" ,
an address, a prefix length and an interface index, and give the
interface that address, permanent, one of at most eight, or take it away;
an index no interface has is
.Er ENODEV ,
a prefix past 128
.Er EINVAL ,
as is a group, :: or ::1 on a card, an address it has already
.Er EEXIST ,
one past the eight
.Er ENOSPC ,
any on an interface whose
.Va disable_ipv6
is 1
.Er EACCES ,
and removing one it does not have
.Er EADDRNOTAVAIL .
.Dv SIOCADDRT
and
.Dv SIOCDELRT
take a
.Vt "struct in6_rtmsg" ,
72 bytes on i386 and 80 on x86-64, and add a route to IPv6's table of
32, through
.Va rtmsg_gateway
when
.Va rtmsg_flags
has
.Dv RTF_GATEWAY ,
by the interface of
.Va rtmsg_ifindex
or, for 0, the one the gateway is reached by, at
.Va rtmsg_metric
or 1024 for 0; or remove the route of that prefix, through that gateway
and by that interface when they are given.
.Ql lo
is
.Er EOPNOTSUPP ,
a route no interface carries
.Er ENODEV ,
and removing one that is not there
.Er ESRCH .
See
.Xr ipv6 7 .
.Pp
.Dv SIOCDARP
takes a 68-byte
.Vt "struct arpreq"
and requires effective user ID 0.
It forgets the ARP mapping of the protocol address, learned on the
interface
.Va arp_dev
names or on any when that is empty, or fails with
.Er ENXIO
when there is none.
.Dv SIOCSARP
stores the mapping of the protocol address to the Ethernet address of
.Va arp_ha ,
on the interface
.Va arp_dev
names or the one whose subnet has the address, permanent with
.Dv ATF_PERM ,
which
.Ql arp -s
sets; a permanent mapping is never changed by a packet nor dropped by a
full cache.
Another kind of hardware address is
.Er EINVAL ,
.Dv ATF_PUBL
.Er EOPNOTSUPP ,
since the stack does not answer for another host, and an address no
interface reaches
.Er ENETUNREACH .
.Pp
The bridge requests,
.Dv SIOCGIFBR ,
.Dv SIOCSIFBR ,
.Dv SIOCBRADDBR
and
.Dv SIOCBRDELBR ,
and the VLAN requests of
.Xr vconfig 1 ,
.Dv SIOCGIFVLAN
and
.Dv SIOCSIFVLAN ,
fail with
.Er ENOPKG ,
as Linux's do when neither module is in its kernel: there are no
bridges or VLANs.
.Ss Socket timestamps
.Bl -tag -width SIOCGSTAMPNS_NEW
.It Dv SIOCGSTAMP , SIOCGSTAMPNS
On an AF_INET, AF_INET6 or packet socket, write the time the datagram a
receive last took or peeked was queued, the one
.Dv SO_TIMESTAMP
reports
.Pq Xr recv 2 ,
in microseconds or nanoseconds.
The old requests, 0x8906 and 0x8907, write the caller's
.Vt struct timeval
or
.Vt struct timespec ,
two 32-bit words from an i386 program on the x86-64 kernel; the new ones,
0x80108906 and 0x80108907, two 64-bit words in every ABI.
Before the socket's first receive, and always on a TCP socket, they fail
with
.Er ENOENT ,
as on Linux; an AF_UNIX or netlink socket does not answer them.
Linux gives the time of the call instead to the first request after a
receive when no socket on the machine has asked for timestamps, since it
then stamps no datagram; Rach2 stamps every one.
.El
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn ioctl
returns 0 or the request's result on success.
On failure it returns -1 and sets
.Va errno .
The kernel interface returns the negative error number.
.Sh EXAMPLES
The default suite's
.Pa tests/jobprobe.c
creates a session, claims the console with
.Dv TIOCSCTTY ,
reads its foreground group with
.Dv TIOCGPGRP ,
and changes that group with
.Dv TIOCSPGRP .
It checks the refusals for a foreign group, a missing, zero or negative
group ID, and a
terminal detached by
.Dv TIOCNOTTY ,
and that once a zombie's group is in front its own orphaned group's
.Dv TIOCSPGRP
fails with
.Er ENOTTY
until it ignores
.Dv SIGTTOU .
.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width "[EOPNOTSUPP]"
.It Bq Er EBADF
.Fa fd
is invalid or was opened with
.Dv O_PATH .
.It Bq Er EBUSY
.Dv BLKRRPART
found another descriptor open on the disk or a claim on its partitions.
A framebuffer request belongs to another process, or a request other
than
.Dv FB_MODE_13H
has no owner.
.It Bq Er EFAULT
A required argument or buffer cannot be read or written in user memory.
.It Bq Er EFBIG
The sector count requested by
.Dv BLKGETSIZE
does not fit 32 bits.
.It Bq Er EINTR
A signal ended a wait, or a terminal request from a background process
group posted
.Dv SIGTTOU .
.It Bq Er EINVAL
The flush selector is outside 0 through 2, the foreground group ID
is not positive, or an interface address, mask, broadcast address, or
gateway is invalid.
Adding a default route with gateway zero is invalid.
The audio driver refused the arguments of
.Dv AUDIO_START :
on HD Audio and the Sound Blaster 16, a rate of 0 or above 192000, an
unknown format, or a channel count other than 1 or 2;
the PC speaker refuses nothing.
.Dv AUDIO_WRITE
was given a zero size, or the console's channel is not playing.
A keyboard request's mode, flag, keymap entry, string or accent count is
out of range.
.It Bq Er EIO
The partition rescan could not read the disk's first sector, the
sound device failed to start the stream of
.Dv AUDIO_START ,
or a terminal request came from an orphaned background process group.
.It Bq Er ENODEV
The named network interface or index does not exist, or an
.Dv AUDIO_*
request other than
.Dv AUDIO_GET_INFO
has no active driver.
.Dv FB_GETINFO
or
.Dv FB_MODE_NATIVE
found no framebuffer, or
.Dv FB_MODE_NATIVE
a framebuffer without a native mode.
.It Bq Er ENOENT
.Dv SIOCGSTAMP
or
.Dv SIOCGSTAMPNS
on a socket that has received no datagram.
.It Bq Er ENOMEM
The kernel had no memory for the console's channel of
.Dv AUDIO_START .
.It Bq Er ENOPKG
A bridge or VLAN request was made.
.It Bq Er ENOTTY
The object does not support the request, a disk size query has no live
raw window, or
.Dv TIOCSPGRP
selected a terminal outside the caller's session or came from an
orphaned background process group.
.It Bq Er EOPNOTSUPP
A clone request found nothing else wrong, a route change other than a
default route or addition of the existing subnet route was requested, or
.Dv AUDIO_WRITE
was given to the PC speaker, which cannot play samples.
.It Bq Er EPERM
A controlling-terminal claim is forbidden, the proposed foreground group
is outside the terminal's session, a network setter or route change
was requested without effective user ID 0, or without privilege over the
caller's network namespace for its
.Ql lo ,
or
.Dv FS_IOC_SETFLAGS
came from a caller that neither owns the file nor is privileged, or
asked an unprivileged caller's change of the immutable or append-only
flag, or a keyboard request came from a caller
.Xr console 4
does not let set it.
.It Bq Er ESRCH
There is no current process,
.Dv TIOCSPGRP
was given zero or a number no thread, process group or session holds,
or the default route to remove is absent
or has a different nonzero gateway from the one supplied.
.El
